---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CriticalityDivider from '../../components/CriticalityDivider.astro';
import InterferometerDiagram from '../../components/InterferometerDiagram.astro';
import phaseResponse from '../../assets/research/cavity_phase_response.png';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Phase interferometry for nanophotonic cavities"
  description="A fibre Mach–Zehnder interferometer that measures the reflection phase of a nanophotonic cavity at room temperature, resolving the over- versus under-coupled regime that reflected intensity alone cannot distinguish."
>
  <article class="shell">
    <p class="back"><a href={base + '/research'}>← Research</a></p>

    <header class="proj-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">2026 · Lukin group, Harvard</p>
      <h1>Phase Interferometry for Room-Temperature Characterization of Nanophotonic Cavities</h1>
      <p class="proj-blurb">
        The reflected <em>intensity</em> of a single-sided cavity cannot tell you whether it is
        over- or under-coupled — both regimes produce the same dip. The reflected
        <em>phase</em> can. This is a fibre Mach–Zehnder interferometer built to measure that
        phase on the bench, at room temperature, before a device is committed to a cryostat.
      </p>

      <dl class="proj-meta">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Visiting Undergraduate Research Fellow</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd class="tags">
            <span class="tag">fibre interferometry</span>
            <span class="tag">cavity QED</span>
            <span class="tag">heterodyne detection</span>
            <span class="tag">AOM phase locking</span>
            <span class="tag">instrument control</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The measurement problem</h2>
      <p>
        A single-sided cavity — one intentional input port with rate <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub>,
        plus unavoidable internal loss <em>κ</em><sub>int</sub> — has a reflection coefficient
        that follows from input–output theory:
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>r</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>ω</em>) = 1 −
        <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub></span><span class="den"><em>i</em>(<em>ω</em> − <em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>) + <em>κ</em>/2</span></span>,
        <span class="eq-where">κ = κ<sub>ext</sub> + κ<sub>int</sub></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        On resonance this collapses to a purely real number whose <em>sign</em> carries the
        physics:
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>r</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>) =
        <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>κ</em><sub>int</sub> − <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub></span><span class="den"><em>κ</em><sub>int</sub> + <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub></span></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        An <strong>over-coupled</strong> cavity (<em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub> &gt; <em>κ</em><sub>int</sub>)
        gives <em>r</em><sub>c</sub> &lt; 0; an <strong>under-coupled</strong> one gives
        <em>r</em><sub>c</sub> &gt; 0. Because a detector measures
        |<em>r</em><sub>c</sub>|<sup>2</sup>, that sign is invisible: a cavity with
        <em>κ</em><sub>ext</sub>/<em>κ</em> = 0.8 and one with 0.2 produce
        <em>identical</em> reflection dips. The phase does not hide it. Sweeping across
        resonance, an over-coupled cavity winds <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub> by a full 2π, while an
        under-coupled cavity makes a small excursion that returns to where it started.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={phaseResponse} alt="Two-panel plot: reflected intensity versus detuning, where over- and under-coupled cavities overlap exactly; and reflection phase versus detuning, where the over-coupled case winds by 2 pi while the under-coupled case barely moves." widths={[600, 1000, 1270]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Computed from the expression above. <strong>Top:</strong> the over- and under-coupled
          traces sit exactly on top of each other — intensity is degenerate. <strong>Bottom:</strong>
          the same two cases are immediately distinguishable in phase. This degeneracy is the
          entire reason the interferometer exists.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="the interferometer" />

      <h2>Reading phase with an unbalanced Mach–Zehnder</h2>
      <p>
        Phase is not directly observable; it has to be converted into intensity by
        interference against a reference. The cavity sits in the signal arm of a fibre
        Mach–Zehnder interferometer; the other arm carries a local oscillator through a
        variable delay. Recombining on a 50:50 coupler gives two complementary outputs,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>I</em><sub>±</sub> ∝ |<em>E</em><sub>S</sub>|<sup>2</sup> + |<em>E</em><sub>L</sub>|<sup>2</sup>
        ± 2|<em>E</em><sub>S</sub>||<em>E</em><sub>L</sub>| cosΔ<em>φ</em>
      </div>

      <p>
        so the <em>difference</em> of the two photodiodes isolates the interference term and
        rejects common-mode intensity noise. The measured phase is a sum of three
        contributions,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        Δ<em>φ</em>(<em>ω</em>) = <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>ω</em>) + <em>ω</em>Δ<em>τ</em> + <em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>
      </div>

      <p>
        of which only the first is wanted. The second is the propagation phase of the arm
        imbalance and the third is a control knob — both have to be dealt with.
      </p>

      <h2>Path matching and the free spectral range</h2>
      <p>
        An arm-length mismatch Δ<em>L</em> contributes a delay
        Δ<em>τ</em> = <em>n</em>Δ<em>L</em>/<em>c</em>, which appears as a linear ramp in
        <em>ω</em>Δ<em>τ</em> — fringes periodic in laser frequency with free spectral range
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        FSR = <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>c</em></span><span class="den"><em>n</em>Δ<em>L</em></span></span>
        ⟹ Δ<em>L</em> = <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>c</em></span><span class="den"><em>n</em>·FSR</span></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        This makes the FSR a <em>ruler</em> for the imbalance: measure the fringe period with
        the cavity detuned away, invert it, and you know how much fibre to add. A metre of
        residual mismatch in fibre (<em>n</em> ≈ 1.47) corresponds to an FSR of a couple of
        hundred MHz, so coarse matching is done by splicing a computed length of fibre and
        the remainder is trimmed with the delay stage. Getting this right matters twice
        over: the ramp must be subtracted to recover <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub> cleanly, and fringe
        visibility itself decays with mismatch once Δ<em>τ</em> approaches the laser coherence
        time,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>V</em> ∝ exp[−(Δ<em>τ</em>/<em>τ</em><sub>c</sub>)<sup>2</sup>]
      </div>

      <p>
        Once the ramp is calibrated out against a reference sweep, the residual is the
        cavity's own phase response, and the winding number reads off the coupling regime.
      </p>

      <CriticalityDivider label="setup" />

      <figure class="fig">
        <InterferometerDiagram />
        <figcaption>
          Schematic of the setup, redrawn from my own sketch. The signal arm carries the probe
          to the cavity through a circulator; the reference arm carries the local oscillator
          through a delay stage and an adjustable attenuator that matches the per-arm power.
          The arms recombine on a 50:50 coupler read out on two photodiodes, whose difference
          drives a servo that holds the fringe at a chosen operating point by feeding a common
          RF phase to the two in-fibre AOMs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Holding the fringe: AOM phase control</h2>
      <p>
        A fibre interferometer drifts — thermal expansion and acoustic pickup move
        Δ<em>φ</em> by many radians on a timescale of seconds, so an unlocked instrument
        measures nothing. The actuators are the two acousto-optic modulators. An AOM driven
        at <em>V</em><sub>RF</sub>(<em>t</em>) = <em>V</em><sub>0</sub>cos(Ω<sub>RF</sub><em>t</em> + <em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>)
        transfers the instantaneous RF phase onto the first-order diffracted beam,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>E</em><sub>1st</sub>(<em>t</em>) ∝ <em>E</em><sub>in</sub>(<em>t</em>)
        <em>e</em><sup><em>i</em>(Ω<sub>RF</sub><em>t</em> + <em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>)</sup>
      </div>

      <p>
        giving a piezo-free, GHz-bandwidth optical phase shifter. Driving the two arms on
        opposite diffraction orders turns one common RF phase into a <em>differential</em>
        optical phase,
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        Δ<em>φ</em><sub>AOM</sub> = (+<em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>) − (−<em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>) = 2<em>δφ</em><sub>AOM</sub>
      </div>

      <p>
        so a single number written to the servo steers the fringe directly. Locking and
        probing are interleaved in time: a frequency-fixed laser holds the lock while a
        tunable laser sweeps the cavity, each gated by its own preparation AOM, so the servo
        never has to follow the probe as it scans.
      </p>

      <h2>What I did</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Worked through the component-level design — what each element contributes and why the arrangement of diffraction orders is what makes the loop closeable.</li>
        <li>Derived the detected signal and the conditions for maximum fringe contrast, including the balanced-detection difference and the power-matching requirement |<em>E</em><sub>S</sub>| = |<em>E</em><sub>L</sub>|.</li>
        <li>Path-matched the arms: measured the FSR, converted it into a physical length, and trimmed the residual with the delay stage.</li>
        <li>Set up and characterised the AOM-based phase lock, and the interleaved lock/probe timing that lets a fixed-frequency reference stabilise a swept measurement.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="note">
        This page describes the method and the physics. Device-specific performance figures
        from the group's unpublished work are deliberately left out.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="proj-foot">
      <a href={base + '/research'}>← Back to research</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .shell { max-width: 44rem; }
  .back { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .back a, .proj-foot a { color: var(--muted); }
  .back a:hover, .proj-foot a:hover { color: var(--accent); }

  .proj-head { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .proj-head h1 { margin: 0.15em 0 0.35em; font-size: var(--step-3); line-height: 1.12; }
  .proj-blurb { font-size: 1.18rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 40rem; }
  .proj-meta {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0; display: grid; gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
  }
  .proj-meta > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; align-items: baseline; }
  .proj-meta dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
  }
  .proj-meta dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem 0.5rem; }
  .tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.74rem;
    color: var(--accent-deep); background: var(--accent-wash);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 3px;
  }

  .fig { margin: 2rem 0; }
  .fig :global(img) {
    display: block; width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--faint); border-radius: 4px; background: #fff;
  }
  .fig figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.6rem; line-height: 1.55;
  }

  /* Display equations — set in the body serif, centred, with a hairline rule. */
  .eq {
    margin: 1.4rem 0;
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .eq em { font-style: italic; }
  .eq-where {
    display: inline-block; margin-left: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted);
  }
  /* inline fraction */
  .frac {
    display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column;
    vertical-align: middle; text-align: center;
    margin: 0 0.15rem;
  }
  .frac .num { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: 0 0.35rem 0.1rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .frac .den { padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem 0; line-height: 1.35; }

  .note {
    margin-top: 2rem; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    background: var(--paper-raised); border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--muted);
  }

  .proj-foot {
    margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .proj-meta > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
  }
</style>
